# The image's build context: an ALLOWLIST, not a denylist.
#
# Without this file `COPY . .` sent the whole working tree into the build stage —
# a developer's articleflux.db and its -wal, secrets.key and the other key files,
# .env with an OpenAI key in it, 150 MB of bin/, e2e/node_modules, every cache
# directory. None of it reached the final image (that stage copies two paths out
# of the build), but all of it sat in a build layer and in the builder's cache,
# which is a worse place for a key than the disk it came from.
#
# A denylist has to name every such file in advance and fails open on the next
# one somebody invents (`*.key` would not have caught `.env.local`). An allowlist
# fails CLOSED: a new top-level directory the build needs is missing, and `go
# build` says so by name. That is the right direction for the failure to run.
*

# The module and everything `go build ./cmd/articleflux` and `make wasm` read.
# migrations/ is not optional: its .sql files are //go:embed'ed into the binary.
# web/ is what `make wasm` assembles bin/web from (index.html, sw.js, fonts,
# manifest, icons, the homepage's screenshots). proto/ is NOT here — the
# generated code is committed under internal/pb and the build never runs buf.
!go.mod
!go.sum
!Makefile
!cmd/
!internal/
!client/
!migrations/
!web/

# .git, for one reason: `go build` stamps vcs.revision into the binary from it,
# and that is the commit the Server tab and `articleflux version` report. Without
# it both say "unknown". 50 MB of context is the price; it is not copied into the
# runtime image.
!.git/

# Re-excluded inside the allowed tree: working state that can land there.
**/*.db
**/*.db-wal
**/*.db-shm
**/*.log.jsonl*
**/*.key
**/.env
